<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Benefits of Multilingualism – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Multilingual Self</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Multilingualism</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain complex ideas with simple examples. Communication: Facilitate diverse group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain a complex idea or concept related to language learning, using a real-life example (e.g., "Learning a new language is like learning a new game—at first, it's hard, but with practice, it becomes more fun and easier to play.")<br>Can facilitate a discussion to clarify concepts by rephrasing them in simpler terms (e.g., "So, what she means is that knowing multiple languages can help you feel more connected to your community.")<br>Can clarify misunderstandings during group discussions about multilingualism (e.g., "I think there is some confusion—being bilingual doesn't just mean speaking two languages well, it also means understanding both cultures better.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by projecting three short, somewhat complex statements about language learning (e.g., "Code-switching is a cognitive exercise that enhances mental flexibility," "Linguistic relativity suggests language shapes thought," "Language acquisition often follows predictable developmental stages"). Students work in pairs to try to rephrase one statement in simpler terms for a hypothetical younger sibling or a non-expert friend. Teacher encourages the use of phrases like "In simpler words..." or "What it means is...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a complex idea about multilingualism (e.g., "The benefits of multilingualism extend beyond communication to cognitive advantages") using a simple analogy: "Learning a new language is like learning a new game—at first, it's hard, but with practice, it becomes more fun and easier to play." The teacher also models using reported speech to share an opinion: "My colleague Ms. Lopez said that multilingualism opens up career opportunities." The teacher explicitly models word stress in "multilingualism" and "language learning."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'Multilingualism enhances cognitive flexibility,' and then I explain it as 'it helps your brain think in new ways,' am I simplifying or making it more complex?" (Expected answer: Simplifying.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I tell you what someone else said, am I using my own words or their reported words?" (Expected answer: Their reported words.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Multilingual Self"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short video or transcript of an interview with a high school student from another country explaining the benefits of being multilingual and how it connects to their identity (Scenario: My Multilingual Self). The student uses simple examples and reported speech. For example, "My grandmother always said that speaking many languages makes you a citizen of the world." The input explicitly includes vocabulary like 'proficiency,' 'communication,' 'career opportunities,' and grammatical structures like reported speech and future simple ("I believe companies will prioritize hiring bilingual employees in the future"). Pronunciation of /s/ in "success" and /z/ in "citizens" is prominent.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand how to explain complex ideas and facilitate discussion by observing a model. They will notice how others discuss language diversity and promote cultural inclusiveness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What was the main idea the student conveyed about multilingualism?" "What was one interesting point the student mentioned about future job opportunities for bilingual individuals?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match reported opinions (e.g., "Her grandmother said...") to the specific benefit of multilingualism mentioned (e.g., "...that it broadens one's perspective of the world.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice explaining complex ideas using simple examples, rephrasing, and clarifying misunderstandings. They will use reported speech (e.g., "She said that multilingualism opens..."), future simple (e.g., "Companies will prioritize..."), and modals of ability (e.g., "You can..."). Vocabulary: multilingualism, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, business, education, fluency, skill; verbs: said, argued, believe, prioritize, offer, communicate, become, excel, enhance, learn; adjectives: bilingual, multilingual, proficient, effective, international, diverse, multicultural, necessary, fluent. Pronunciation practice with /s/ and /z/ sounds, and word stress in "multilingualism," "multicultural," and "language learning."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Concept Simplification):</b> Teacher provides 5-6 complex sentences related to "The Benefits of Multilingualism" (e.g., "Cognitive advantages are significantly enhanced through the acquisition of multiple linguistic systems.") and asks students to rewrite them in simpler, more accessible language for a general audience. Students use target vocabulary and grammar. (e.g., "Learning many languages can enhance your brain's skills.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Rephrasing for Clarity):</b> In pairs, students receive cards with common misunderstandings or complex statements about multilingualism (e.g., "Being bilingual just means you speak two languages perfectly," or "Multilingualism is only useful for international business."). One student reads a statement, and the other student rephrases it to clarify, using phrases like "I think there is some confusion—being bilingual doesn't just mean speaking two languages well, it also means understanding both cultures better." or "So, what she means is that knowing multiple languages can help you feel more connected to your community." They are encouraged to use reported speech to refer to general opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Infographic Explanation Practice):</b> Students work in groups of three. Student A acts as a presenter, explaining a specific section or benefit from their planned "Multilingualism Infographic" to Student B. Student B then summarizes Student A's points for Student C, clarifying any complex terms and using reported speech ("She said that improved communication is a major benefit..."). Student C asks a clarifying question, and Student A or B rephrases the answer. Teacher circulates, providing feedback on mediation strategies and pronunciation, especially /s/, /z/ and word stress.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Multilingual Self"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Presentation and Facilitated Discussion of "Multilingualism Infographic".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students present their "Multilingualism Infographic" in small groups (3-4 students). Each student takes a turn explaining a key section of their infographic, focusing on its main benefits. Their role is to mediate the information for their peers, explaining complex ideas with simple examples, rephrasing statements for clarity, and anticipating potential misunderstandings. They must incorporate reported speech to highlight diverse opinions on language learning (e.g., "My mother always said that language proficiency enhances career opportunities," or "A recent article argued that companies will prioritize hiring bilingual employees in the future."). They also use future simple for predictions and modals of ability. After each explanation, one student from the group acts as a facilitator, guiding a brief discussion (2-3 minutes) and ensuring everyone understands the concepts, clarifying any points as needed, demonstrating cultural inclusiveness, and offering practical, culturally sensitive advice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes students during their infographic presentations and facilitated discussions. Each student is assessed on their ability to explain a complex idea (from their infographic), rephrase a point, and clarify a potential misunderstanding during the group discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clearly explains complex ideas with simple examples.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectively rephrases concepts or questions for better understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarifies misunderstandings during group discussions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses reported speech, future simple, and modals of ability accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Utilizes target vocabulary (multilingualism, proficiency, opportunities, etc.) appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /s/ in words like "success" and /z/ in "citizens" is intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates appropriate word stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "multilingualism").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Promotes cultural inclusiveness and offers culturally sensitive advice.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one idea from our infographic that I explained well using a simple example?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I help clarify a point or overcome a misunderstanding in our group discussion today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the most important mediation skill I learned or improved today (e.g., explaining, clarifying, rephrasing), and how will I use it outside of English class?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has creating and presenting the 'Multilingualism Infographic' helped me understand the topic more deeply and communicate its benefits effectively?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"The activities successfully engaged students in explaining and clarifying concepts related to multilingualism. The infographic project served as an excellent vehicle for mediation. Students effectively incorporated reported speech and future simple. Some students struggled with natural rephrasing without sounding repetitive; more explicit sentence frames for clarification might be beneficial next time. Pronunciation practice for /s/ and /z/ in context was well-received.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "The Benefits of Multilingualism" and the "Multilingualism Infographic" project. The next sequence will introduce a new theme and scenario, building on students' developed communication and mediation skills to explore new contexts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>